Your Closures page uses `makeAdder` as its example of a closure. makeAdder is a function factory, and it creates functions that can add a specific value to their argument.[1]

```js
function makeAdder(x) {
  return function (y) {
    return x + y;
  };
}

const add5 = makeAdder(5);
const add10 = makeAdder(10);

console.log(add5(2)); // 7
console.log(add10(2)); // 12
```

The inner function returns the sum of the `x` it was built with and the `y` it receives later. add5 and add10 both form closures. They share the same function body definition, but store different lexical environments.[2] So `x` is 5 inside `add5` and 10 inside `add10`, which is why the two calls print different results.

