You can keep a variable private by putting it inside a function and returning only the functions that should be able to change it. This is the closure pattern. The passages show a counter where the variable `privateCounter` and the function `changeBy` live inside an anonymous function that runs as soon as it is defined.[1]

```javascript
let privateCounter = 0;
  function changeBy(val) {
    privateCounter += val;
  }
```

Code outside that wrapper has no direct way to reach the variable. The public functions returned from the wrapper are the only indirect route to these private members.[2] Those returned functions keep access to the variable, because they form closures that share the same lexical environment.[3]

Your library also notes that this approach provides benefits that are normally associated with object-oriented programming. In particular, data hiding and encapsulation.[4]

