**Closures in JavaScript.** Your notes describe a closure as a function kept together with the variables around it, so the inner function can still reach them after the outer function has returned. Your `makeAdder` example shows this: each returned function remembers its own `x`. add5 and add10 both form closures. They share the same function body definition, but store different lexical environments.[1]

**Function literals in Go.** Your Effective Go notes say the same idea applies in Go. In Go, function literals are closures: the implementation makes sure the variables referred to by the function survive as long as they are active.[2] A common use is with goroutines, which your notes say can be handy in a goroutine invocation.[3] Your example is:

```go
func Announce(message string, delay time.Duration) {
    go func() {
        time.Sleep(delay)
        fmt.Println(message)
    }()  // Note the parentheses - must call the function.
}
```

The `go` keyword starts a call in a new goroutine. When the call completes, the goroutine exits, silently.[4] The trailing `()` is needed because the literal has to be called.

